            <ArticleTitle>Air quality and pediatric asthma-related emergencies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>839-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Previous studies suggest a relationship between air pollutants, aeroallergens, and asthma exacerbations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To simultaneously examine the role of seasonality, air quality, aeroallergens, and climate on asthma-related pediatric emergency department (ED) visits.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective 4-year study of asthma-related ED visits was conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">September had the highest number of visits (p &lt; 0.01). There were lower temperatures and precipitation (p &lt; 0.01) and higher tree and weed pollen levels (p = 0.05) on days with more visits (p = 0.05), while grass pollen, mold, ozone, NO2, and PM2.5 levels showed no significant differences.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Asthma-related visits were associated with aeroallergens and climatic factors and not air-quality factors.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
